{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/786d223be90c13335ab80672631dcbef25297688ae9e318928e744ebbf8a5cdf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I is true, and II and III are false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48185" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-48185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48185" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/4a9a1be6abdaf5104cecf7544dc77687db2892de1e3b39faab03f99b84003c03.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$\n- It has a point discontinuity at $x=2.$\n- It is continuous at $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I is true, and II and III are false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-38712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/034e2813b404338a1b8d0ecfdb509781f7917e356155474d788d8bd85df0f221.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true, and I and III are false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48187" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"questi