its both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-48141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48141" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/49ecc13120246d04cd45e3ff209381b71f0b69dd0d53258f9b9606826b9eb372.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/6f632ae4f606f49b89490457e7310f5dd949f3e2a66b9ad9a8268911af3b7db5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8961fba5e