 we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-104689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-2$ and $x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=-2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=2$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/adec7c5c587dcaf899d53432f724a52c0b59d40dfc9d5fafc6cfca92ca3e052e.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-1$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-73046" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-1$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It's continuous at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, I is true, while II and III are false." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=7?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x\\le 7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30, & x>7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<7 \\\\ 5x+14, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}, & x<7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(7)=2(7)-1=13$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}(2x-1)=2(7)-1=13\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}({x}^{2}-30)={7}^{2}-30=19.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(7)=5(7)+14=49$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}{x}^{2}=49, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}(5x+14)=5(7)+14=49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=49.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,7}g(x)=49=g(7).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(7)=\\frac{1}{7}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-7}=-\\infty \n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nSince the left limit is infinite, $h(x)$ does not have a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104701" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}x+4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1, & x>1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1+4=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(x+4)=1+4=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}+1)=3(1)^{2}+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(1)=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+3})=\\sqrt{1+3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=2=g(1).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}h(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\ne h(1).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=4?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5, & x<4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5-2x, & x\\le 4 \\\\ x+3, & x>4\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 5, & x=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-104693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "34e8faa35fdb84b2df88f51d451b94aac251936d9b351dea5e31026c65f40fa0"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "q-48114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$h(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/state.json" "file_sha256" "1173701a3cdf83ac4300d11b90214044dda01ea48bca1b8cbcc2fff4a826c8b5"} {"question" "e-3582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3582.json" "file_sha256" "ca94c525b4271e58be629d5971f953d4d80b449cfbe77352727a25ff51c547d2"} {"question" "e-3582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3582.json" "file_sha256" "ca94c525b4271e58be629d5971f953d4d80b449cfbe77352727a25ff51c547d2"} {"question" "e-3583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/9e708e4fea2cd98e8d76978e58a4525d610d6f82521345b3aaa6232fd39b7539.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3583.json" "file_sha256" "f1e8330d3ce08136caaae8e0854fed0b98099ddb4381e75e5f8ac5d2ba748b8b"} {"question" "e-3583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3583.json" "file_sha256" "f1e8330d3ce08136caaae8e0854fed0b98099ddb4381e75e5f8ac5d2ba748b8b"} {"question" "e-3671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Does the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/49e4cd87824a20b73175305aac7f3773f04a28e02cfa9c3c89cef9079a21ae8a.png) below have a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/22a0edba1f275de80719ea33fc4878640d2b2b93f8e5620c8999d6a449cdd35b.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/742066aa035c1b710ab30a74ff5320d3941aae17bbbb8a4c5edcf35ff67c5920.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3671.json" "file_sha256" "de40c701330aa2b9a8a91cf546cf59b0a2688af1afff0f4eeb1dadf7d3e30b7e"} {"question" "e-3671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/29d8d36954a25bff5808ce7c59dfc3ec0e373d22541cc2df85c93aa0a74c8bb4.png) exists ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/a4785336954b0736113ea8ad968aa60db31e55720ff5bdeb128597a8c7c72b90.png)\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/949d5bef0569fbd6f58382e07873412bb07cf3b735ae264c9cb78b366fa59bd2.png)\nwhile\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/d6dfbdbb30c840068ad7a86370f6e444453f925ba8869140b7288b51d164f228.png)\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/81510388d37345ec3bfb525da6795c62d4ef190798e1574139469b5a90d17d69.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/1d1ac18ca3b92f6df4647c49855e18d40ab7637269ff9b366edbfcaa96c4d59d.png) has a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8f57be1d6e33e5ee1ad485bc58b29dbca4819ee229a505c0e7601150392d7615.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/example-3671.json" "file_sha256" "de40c701330aa2b9a8a91cf546cf59b0a2688af1afff0f4eeb1dadf7d3e30b7e"}] "usage" {"e-3583" [] "q-48119" [] "e-3671" [] "q-48141" [] "e-3582" []} "basis" 1443 "retractions" [[17592186129739 :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x =-1$?"] [17592186129736 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48119.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?"] [17592186129736 :question/answer-fields 17592186128210] [17592186128838 :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/145658c34dbb00034b6a487c5e77e888.png)"] [17592186128838 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$. Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3$, but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities."] [17592186128839 :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/c6c40035c03ae2873bb952c1aba81138.png)"] [17592186128839 :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is not a jump discontinuity."] [17592186128848 :question/problem "Does the function $f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity at $x=0$?\n\n${x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ 2x - 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$"] [17592186128848 :question/worked-solution "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^-} 2x-1 = 2(0)-1 = -1$\nwhile\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^+} x^2+1 = 0^2+1 = 1$.\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186128211 17592186128212 17592186128213 17592186128214 17592186128215 17592186128229 17592186128230 17592186128231 17592186128232 17592186128233] "immutable_fields" [[17592186128210 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128210 :answer-field/id] [17592186128210 :answer-field/key] [17592186128210 :answer-field/type] [17592186128228 :answer-field/correct] [17592186128228 :answer-field/id] [17592186128228 :answer-field/key] [17592186128228 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
